            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Seth P. Waxman, the case involves Robert Leroy McCoy, who faces the death penalty, and raises the question of whether a trial court may allow defense counsel to tell a jury that the defendant is guilty over the defendant\u2019s objection."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Louisiana",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "cfb0236509cbac0f2ef6b5008b3a725f97c911a1377a709f9f65a4c628902eba",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-cfb0236509cb-0001-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 32,
              "start": 32
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Louisiana, it also asks whether counsel may be required to override a client\u2019s strategy in a narrow class of death\u2011penalty cases when that strategy is futile, and whether such an override constitutes ineffective assistance of counsel."
      }
    ],
