            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Chief Justice Roberts asked whether a simple statement that the client shot the victim necessarily means the client is guilty."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "cfb0236509cbac0f2ef6b5008b3a725f97c911a1377a709f9f65a4c628902eba",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-cfb0236509cb-0001-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 26,
              "start": 26
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Justice Breyer asked whether a lawyer\u2019s explicit statement that the defendant is guilty to the jury would be prohibited by the Sixth Amendment."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "cfb0236509cbac0f2ef6b5008b3a725f97c911a1377a709f9f65a4c628902eba",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-cfb0236509cb-0002-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 46,
              "start": 46
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Justice Gorsuch asked whether the defendant effectively conceded guilt to second\u2011degree murder."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "cfb0236509cbac0f2ef6b5008b3a725f97c911a1377a709f9f65a4c628902eba",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-cfb0236509cb-0002-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 46,
              "start": 46
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Justice Sotomayor asked whether there was mens rea for both first\u2011 and second\u2011degree murder, noting the defendant\u2019s argument for only second\u2011degree."
      }
    ],
